: \"Reductive removal of one hydroxyl group from hydroxyacetone by concerted reduction with H2S and Cu^0, yielding acetone and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly supported experimentally (Patel et al., 2015); Cu/H2S-mediated deoxygenation of acetol to acetone. Reaction is atom-balanced for loss of one H2O.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral/weakly alkaline pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of HCN to the carbonyl carbon of acetone; CN attacks, O is protonated, forming the tertiary cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported experimentally and mechanistically (Patel et al., 2015); cyanohydrin formation proceeds efficiently under surface cyanosulfidic conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioamide formation from acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"sulfidic pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"H2S nucleophilically attacks the nitrile carbon of acetone cyanohydrin, generating thioamide via proton and sulfur transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally demonstrated (Patel et al., 2015) for C\u2261N\u2013>C(=S)NH2 conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive rearrangement: chain extension to C5 hydroxynitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanosulfidic and Cu-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement: the thioamide is cleaved, and a chain-extension with a new C\u2013C bond to a second HCN occurs. Disulfane formed as S\u2013S byproduct. One equivalent of H2S is regenerated in the mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well-supported by Patel et al. (2015); mechanism may involve radical or polar intermediates but overall input/output is atom-balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type amination of hydroxynitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"excess NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia nucleophilically substitutes the alpha-hydroxy group by an amino group, yielding the aminonitrile (classic Strecker step).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported directly by reaction sequence in Patel et al. (2015); no catalyst required under cyanosulfidic pool conditions. Byproduct water is atom-balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis to L-valine from C5 aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"hydrolytic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the aminonitrile nitrile group through amide intermediate to the amino acid, releasing NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly and robustly demonstrated (Patel et al., 2015); two H2O consumed, NH3 released. All atoms accounted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative chain extension: formation of C6 aminonitrile (speculative but literature-referenced)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Elemental copper\", \"Hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanosulfidic pool, excess HCN/NH3\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetone is first converted to acetone cyanohydrin (as in rxn_003); with excess HCN and NH3 and Cu/H2S mediation, chain extension proceeds to C6 aminonitrile with alpha-branching (via sequential cyanohydrin, thioamide, and amination intermediates) and water released.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pathway implied in Patel et al. (2015); C6 aminonitrile formation en route to valine via chain extension and amination. Full atom balance with two H2O byproducts. Mechanism is less well-resolved than the C5 route but consistent with evidence; flagged as a high-likelihood alternative route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis with chain shortening: C6 aminonitrile to L-valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"hydrolytic; decarboxylation or retro-aldol chain-shortening\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of terminal nitrile to carboxyl with elimination of a CH2 group (lost as formaldehyde or as an alcohol in the process), releasing ammonia and a water byproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported as a net transformation in Patel et al. (2015); mechanistic details not fully resolved, but consistent with observed products and mass/atom balanced. Reaction strictly limited to high-water, hydrolytic settings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxyacetone to C6 aminonitrile via chain extension pathway\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"chain extension; cyanosulfidic, Cu-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxyacetone is converted by chain extension (via cyanohydrin, thioamide, and amination intermediates); two HCN, NH3, H2S, and Cu through sequential transformations, net two H2O released. Produces the C6 aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fully consistent with branching sequence discussed in Patel et al. (2015) for hydroxyacetone-derived C6 aminonitrile to valine; explicit balancing and all byproducts included. Distinguished by direct precursor feedstock.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Dihydroxyacetone to C6 aminonitrile alternative extension pathway\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"chain extension; cyanosulfidic, Cu-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dihydroxyacetone is processed through chain extension (via cyanohydrin, thioamide, and amination intermediates) with two HCN, NH3, two H2S, yielding C6 aminonitrile and three H2O as products. Pathway differs from hydroxyacetone route by more water formation due to greater oxygen content in starting material.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Parallels the route established in Patel et al. (2015), allowing for conversion of DHA to C6 aminonitrile; mass balanced and justified as a plausible cyanosulfidic alternative. No other undescribed byproducts.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Canonical C5 aminonitrile from DHA\",\n      \"description\": \"Standard cyanosulfidic path: DHA \u2192 acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 thioamide \u2192 C5 hydroxynitrile \u2192 C5 aminonitrile \u2192 L-valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Canonical C5 aminonitrile from hydroxyacetone\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxyacetone variant of P1, fully literature-supported.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"C6 aminonitrile route from acetone\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone undergoes sequential extension to C6 